<p>This indicates a <strong>stack</strong> is the ideal data structure for =
storing the candidates list, since stacks can be used to efficiently mainta=
in a <strong>monotonic decreasing order</strong> of values, as mentioned in=
 the introduction.</p>
<p>The top of the stack represents the most recent candidate to the right o=
f each new number encountered. Given this, here=E2=80=99s how to use the st=
ack to add and remove candidates at each value:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Pop off all candidates from the top of the stack less than or equal to t=
he current value.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The top of the stack will then represent the next largest number of the =
current value.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Record the top of the stack as the answer for the current value.</li>
<li>If the stack is empty, there=E2=80=99s no next largest number for the c=
urrent value. So, record -1.</li>
</ul>
<ol start=3D"3">
<li>Add the current value as a new candidate by pushing it to the top of th=
e stack.</li>
</ol>
